    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d213">

                <pb facs="0266" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Japan)/6–1846: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smyth</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-06-18T09:00:00+09:00">June 18, 1946—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 18—7:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1001. ReEmbtel 745, May 3.<anchor n="24" xml:id="d213-f1"/> 67th Division
                    (formerly honored Second Division of 53rd Army) has been designated basic unit
                    of Chinese occupation force to be sent to Japan. Total strength this force,
                    supplemented by necessary service personnel, is 12,367 officers and men. Units
                    this force concentrated Shanghai, Kowloon and Haiphong and will depart for Japan
                    upon receipt notice from SCAP and arrival of
                    ships for transportation.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d213-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Smyth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d213-f1" n="24" xml:id="d213-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see footnote 9, <ref target="#pg238">p. 238</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d213-f2" n="25" xml:id="d213-f2-content">
                <p>An exchange of notes confirming arrangements for the Chinese occupation force in
                    Japan was finally completed; see the note of October 4 to the Chinese
                    Ambassador, <ref target="#pg329">p. 329</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
